# unknown-escape.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M98b/M98d): a string escape outside the supported set —
# demonstrates `tflw check`'s teaching diagnostic (TF047: lexer, tflw M98b / review `A1-05`, widened
# by M98d / D166).
#
# Supported: `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, and `\u{XXXX}`. Everything else is an error.
#
# `"^\d+$"` used to decode to `^d+$` — the backslash silently dropped — and the run then matched
# against a pattern nobody wrote, printing the written form in the step echo and the mangled form in
# the reason line without ever connecting the two.
#
# **An error rather than a preserved backslash, and that is the permanent choice.** Preserving it is
# what a regex author wants; but under that rule the meaning of `"\q"` depends on membership in a
# small table, so every escape added to the table later would silently change the value of existing
# suites. A rejected program becoming legal is additive. The reverse is not — and additive is the
# only direction a frozen surface can move. JS, Java and non-raw Python all land here. In a regular
# expression, write the backslash twice.
#
# The last two lines are M98d's widening: `\u{XXXX}` is the escape hatch TF049 needs in order to be a
# lint rather than a removed capability, and every way of getting it wrong reports this same code —
# no braces (what a JS author's fingers produce), no code point, unclosed, above `\u{10FFFF}`, or
# naming a surrogate half. One code widened rather than a second one added, because the *fix* is the
# same in every case: spell the escape the way tflw spells it. Contrast `TF003`, which M98c split
# precisely because re-indenting a block and changing an editor setting are unrelated repairs.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/unknown-escape.tflw

test "a regular expression written with a single backslash"
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.id matches "^\d+$"

test "the JS spelling of a unicode escape, which tflw does not accept"
  api GET /health
  expect body.name equals "\u0041"
